ion from a fluorescence scan of a voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>27</volume>:<fpage>585</fpage>&#x02013;595.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11055440</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Glauner, K.S., L.M. Mannuzzu, C.S. Gandhi, and E.Y. Isacoff. <year>1999</year>. Spectroscopic mapping of voltage sensor movement in the <italic>Shaker</italic> potassium channel. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>402</volume>:<fpage>813</fpage>&#x02013;817.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10617202</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Hancox, J.C., A.J. Levi, and H.J. Witchel. <year>1998</year>. Time course and voltage dependence of expressed HERG current compared with native &#x0201c;rapid&#x0201d; delayed rectifier K current during the cardiac ventricular action potential. <source>Pflugers Arch.</source> <volume>436</volume>:<fpage>843</fpage>&#x02013;853.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9799397</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Heginbotham, L., and R. MacKinnon. <year>1992</year>. The aromatic binding site for tetraethylammonium ion on potassium channels. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>483</fpage>&#x02013;491.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1550673</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Hidalgo, P., and R. MacKinnon. <year>1995</year>. Revealing the architecture of a K<sup>+</sup> channel pore through mutant cycles with a peptide inhibitor. <source>Science.</source> <volume>268</volume>:<fpage>307</fpage>&#x02013;310.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7716527</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Hoshi, T., W.N. Zagotta, and R.W. Aldrich. <year>1991</year>. Two types of inactivation on Shaker K<sup>+</sup> channels: effects of alterations in the carboxy-terminal region. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>7</volume>:<fpage>547</fpage>&#x02013;556.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1931050</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Klemic, K.G., C.-C. Shieh, G.E. Kirsch, and S.W. Jones. <year>1998</year>. Inactivation of Kv2.1 potassium channels. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>74</volume>:<fpage>1779</fpage>&#x02013;1789.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9545040</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Larsson, H.P., and F. Elinder. <year>2000</year>. A conserved glutamate is important for slow inactivation in K<sup>+</sup> channels. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>27</volume>:<fpage>573</fpage>&#x02013;583.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11055439</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Li-Smerin, Y., D.H. Hackos, and K.J. Swartz. <year>2000</year>. &#x003b1;-Helical structure elements within the voltage-sensing domains of a K<sup>+</sup> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>115</volume>:<fpage>33</fpage>&#x02013;49.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10613917</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Liu, Y., M.E. Jurman, and G. Yellen. <year>1996</year>. Dynamic rearrangement of the outer mouth of a K<sup>+</sup> channel during gating. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>859</fpage>&#x02013;867.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8608004</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Lopez-Barneo, J., T. Hoshi, S.H. Heinemann, and R.W. Aldrich. <year>1993</year>. Effects of external cations and mutations in the pore region on C-type inactivation of Shaker potassium channels. <source>Receptors Channels.</source> <volume>1</volume>:<fpage>61</fpage>&#x02013;71.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8081712</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Miller, C. <year>1995</year>. The charybdotoxin family of K<sup>+</sup> channel-blocking peptides. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>5</fpage>&#x02013;10.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7542463</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Mitcheson, J.S., J. Chen, and M.C. Sanguinetti. <year>2000</year>. Trapping of a methanesulfonanilide by closure of the HERG potassium channel activation gate. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>115</volume>:<fpage>229</fpage>&#x02013;239.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10694252</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Ortega-Saenz, P., R. Pardal, A. Castellano, and J. Lopez-Barneo. <year>2000</year>. Collapse of conductance is prevented by a glutamate residue conserved in voltage-dependent K<sup>+</sup> channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>116</volume>:<fpage>181</fpage>&#x02013;190.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10919865</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Panyi, G., Z.-F. Sheng, L.-W. Tu, and C. Deutsch. <year>1995</year>. C-type inactivation of a voltage-gated K<sup>+</sup> channel occurs by a cooperative mechanism. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>69</volume>:<fpage>896</fpage>&#x02013;903.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8519989</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Pardo-Lopez, L., M. Zhang, J. Liu, M. Jiang, L.D. Possani, and G.-N. Tseng. <year>2002</year>. Mapping the binding site of a HERG-specific peptide toxin (ErgTx) to the channel's outer vestibule. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>277</volume>:<fpage>16403</fpage>&#x02013;16411.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11864985</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Rasmusson, R.L., M.J. Morales, R.C. Castellino, Y. Zhang, D.L. Campbell, and H.C. Strauss. <year>1995</year>. C-type inactivation controls recovery in a fast inactivating cardiac K<sup>+</sup> channel (Kv1.4) expressed in <italic>Xenopus</italic> oocytes. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>489</volume>:<fpage>709</fpage>&#x02013;721.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8788936</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Rasmusson, R.L., M.J. Morales, S. Wang, S. Liu, D.L. Campbell, M.V. Brahmajothi, and H.C. Strauss. <year>1998</year>. Inactivation of voltage-gated cardiac K<sup>+</sup> channels. <source>Circ. Res.</source> <volume>82</volume>:<fpage>739</fpage>&#x02013;750.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9562433</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Sanguinetti, M.C., C. Jiang, M.E. Curran, and M.T. Keating. <year>1995</year>. A mechanistic link between an inherited and an acquired cardiac arrhythmia: HERG encodes the I<sub>Kr</sub> potassium channel. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>81</volume>:<fpage>299</fpage>&#x02013;307.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7736582</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Schreibmayer, W., H.A. Lester, and N. Dascal. <year>1994</year>. Voltage clamping of <italic>Xenopus</italic> laevis oocytes utilizing agarose-cushion electrodes. <source>Pflugers Arch.</source> <volume>426</volume>:<fpage>453</fpage>&#x02013;458.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7517034</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Smith, P.L., T. Baukrowitz, and G. Yellen. <year>1996</year>. The inward rectification mechanism of the HERG cardiac potassium channel. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>379</volume>:<fpage>833</fpage>&#x02013;836.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8587608</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Spector, P.S., M.E. Curran, A. Zou, M.T. Keating, and M.C. Sanguinetti. <year>1996</year>. Fast inactivation causes rectification of the I<sub>Kr</sub> channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>107</volume>:<fpage>611</fpage>&#x02013;619.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8740374</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Splawski, I., J. Shen, K.W. Timothy, M.H. Lehmann, S.G. Priori, J.L. Robinson, A.J. Moss, P.J. Schwartz, J.A. Towbin, G.M. Vincent, and M.T. Keating. <year>2000</year>. Spectrum of mutations in long-QT syndrome genes <italic>KvLQT1</italic>, <italic>HERG</italic>, <italic>SCN5A</italic>, <italic>KCNE1</italic>, and <italic>KCNE2</italic>. <source>Circulation.</source> <volume>102</volume>:<fpage>1178</fpage>&#x02013;1185.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10973849</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Starkus, J.G., L. Kuschel, M.D. Rayner, and S.H. Heinemann. <year>1997</year>. Ion conduction through C-type inactivated <italic>Shaker</italic> channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>110</volume>:<fpage>539</fpage>&#x02013;550.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9348326</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Tseng-Crank, J.C.L., G.-N. Tseng, A. Schwartz, and M.A. Tanouye. <year>1990</year>. Molecular cloning and functional expression of a potassium channel cDNA isolated from a rat cardiac library. <source>FEBS Lett.</source> <volume>268</volume>:<fpage>63</fpage>&#x02013;68.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2384173</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">Tseng, G.-N. <year>2001</year>. I<sub>Kr</sub>: the hERG channel. <source>J. Mol. Cell. Cardiol.</source> <volume>33</volume>:<fpage>835</fpage>&#x02013;849.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11343409</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="journal">Yellen, G., D. Sodickson, T.-Y. Chen, and M.E. Jurman. <year>1994</year>. An engineered cysteine in the external mouth of a K<sup>+</sup> channel allows inactivation to be modulated by metal binding. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>66</volume>:<fpage>1068</fpage>&#x02013;1075.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8038379</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Zhou, J., Q. Gong, B. Ye, Z. Fan, J.C. Makielski, G.A. Robertson, and C.T. January. <year>1998</year>. Properties of HERG channels stably expressed in HEK 293 cells studied at physiological temperature. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>74</volume>:<fpage>230</fpage>&#x02013;241.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9449325</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>*</label><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> AP, action potential&#x0003b; HERG, human ether-a-go-go&#x02013;related gene&#x0003b; MTS, methanethiosulfonate&#x0003b; WT, wild-type.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>